1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:04,120
You sent a message on WhatsApp
today without thinking about it.

2
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:11,580
Back in 2014, the machine behind that
little tap was moving 40 billion messages a

3
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:16,200
day. Two million live
connections on a single server.

4
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:21,340
Around 32 engineers running all of it.

5
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:26,240
A team that small should not have
been able to move that much.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,760
So how does so little run so much?

7
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:34,700
Follow one message from the
moment your thumb hits send.

8
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:40,000
Your phone isn't making a
fresh request for every text.

9
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:43,500
It holds one persistent TCP connection.

10
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:50,800
A single long-lived socket, opened
once and kept open for hours.

11
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:53,610
No reconnecting for every message.

12
00:00:54,580 --> 00:00:56,900
But a warm pipe has a price.

13
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:04,640
Every open socket is memory and a file
handle the server has to hold even when

14
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:05,960
nothing is being said.

15
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:13,780
Multiply that by hundreds of millions
of mostly idle phones, then just

16
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:17,520
keeping the connection
alive becomes the hard part.

17
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:26,380
Before the message even leaves, it gets
squeezed down into a compact binary protocol

18
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:33,340
called Fan XMPP, swapping verbose
tags for single byte tokens to

19
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:34,920
save bandwidth and battery.

20
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:41,060
The slimmed down message travels up that
socket and lands inside a process that

21
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:42,660
exists only for you.

22
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:46,140
This is the heart of the design.

23
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:49,160
It's called the actor model.

24
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:55,740
The backend was built almost entirely in
Erlang, a language that came out of the

25
00:01:55,800 --> 00:02:01,500
telecom world. Every connection becomes
its own lightweight process on the BEAM

26
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:08,320
virtual machine. Not a thread, not a request
handler, just one tiny isolated worker

27
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:12,200
per phone, alive for as long
as your circuit stays open.

28
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:16,420
That isolation is everything.

29
00:02:17,580 --> 00:02:24,520
When one process crashes, it dies on its
own and a supervisor respawns a clean

30
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:28,180
one instead of taking the
whole box down with it.

31
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:31,740
Erlang engineers call this let it crash.

32
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:33,920
None of it comes free, though.

33
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:40,700
Erlang is a niche language with a hiring
pool to match, and it was never built for

34
00:02:40,780 --> 00:02:42,280
raw number crunching speed.

35
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:47,720
What it was built for is holding millions
of things at once without falling over.

36
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:50,240
That was the trade they made.

37
00:02:51,980 --> 00:02:53,940
Now, the message needs a destination.

38
00:02:54,920 --> 00:03:00,880
Your process asks one question: Where
does the recipient live right now?

39
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:08,400
The answer sits in Mnesia, Erlang's
in-memory distributed database.

40
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:14,140
It holds the routing table that
maps every user to their server.

41
00:03:14,980 --> 00:03:18,680
Around two terabytes of
it, all kept in RAM.

42
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:25,660
An in-memory lookup is fast enough to run
on every single message, which is exactly

43
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:28,280
what routing tens of
billions of them demands.

44
00:03:29,540 --> 00:03:36,070
But RAM is expensive and an in-memory
store has to be replicated carefully,

45
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:40,420
so they traded durability
and cost for speed.

46
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:46,020
And one message isn't always
headed to just one person.

47
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:51,200
Send it to a group and your phone
still uploads it exactly once.

48
00:03:51,890 --> 00:03:55,040
A single copy tagged with a group ID.

49
00:03:56,240 --> 00:03:58,200
The expansion happens on the server.

50
00:03:59,320 --> 00:04:03,980
It looks up every member and queues
a separate copy for each one.

51
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:11,220
This is server-side fan-out, and it is
why the numbers never quite add up.

52
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:19,399
On the 2nd of April 2014, 20 billion messages
people sent fanned out into 44 billion

53
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:23,240
delivered. 64 billion in a single day.

54
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:31,080
That gap is fan-out made visible and the
cost scales with the size of the group, not

55
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:32,640
how many people are typing.

56
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:37,780
Either way, the message
still has to reach a person.

57
00:04:38,620 --> 00:04:44,640
If they are online, your process hands it
straight to DS and it flows right down

58
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:49,300
their open socket. The service
never let two phones talk directly.

59
00:04:49,960 --> 00:04:55,220
Everything routes through the middle
in a pattern called store and forward.

60
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:58,320
But the recipient isn't always there.

61
00:04:59,300 --> 00:05:01,800
Phones die. Tunnels swallow the signal.

62
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:07,300
People fall asleep. If no one's there to
receive it, the message drops into an

63
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:12,300
encrypted offline queue and
waits for up to 30 days.

64
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:16,500
Come back inside that
window and it drains.

65
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:19,420
Come back after and it's gone.

66
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:22,620
The server is a relay, not a mailbox.

67
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:27,160
And those little ticks you watch
are that relay reporting back.

68
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:30,140
One gray tick means the server has it.

69
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:33,960
Two gray tick means it reached the device.

70
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:37,480
Two blue tick means it has been read.

71
00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:41,940
The ticks are the architecture
leaking through onto your screen.

72
00:05:43,460 --> 00:05:46,700
Up to now, the server
has known everything.

73
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:52,200
Where you live, when you reconnect,
which queue is holding your backlog.

74
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:55,680
Encryption is where that knowing stops.

75
00:05:56,840 --> 00:06:02,720
In November 2014, WhatsApp partnered with
Open Whisper SystemsThe people behind the

76
00:06:02,780 --> 00:06:08,840
Signal Protocol. And by April 2016, end-to-end
encryption was on by default for over

77
00:06:08,860 --> 00:06:15,010
a billion users. A pre-key handshake called
X3V helps two people agree on a shared

78
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:18,060
key, even when one of them is offline.

79
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:26,160
Then the double ratchet takes over, deriving
a fresh key for every single message.

80
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:29,360
So stealing one key
never unlocks the rest.

81
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:37,920
Now, the routing, the queue, and the fanout
all move ciphertext the server can't

82
00:06:38,060 --> 00:06:40,900
read. It knows where to send the envelope.

83
00:06:41,740 --> 00:06:43,560
It just never learns what's inside.

84
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:46,140
But that blindness has a bill.

85
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:51,640
A server that can't read your messages
can't search them, can't scan them for spam,

86
00:06:52,080 --> 00:06:54,940
and can't rebuild your
history on a new device.

87
00:06:57,480 --> 00:07:03,020
That sealed envelope assumed one key person,
and these days people live on more than

88
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:09,940
one device. So in 2021, Meta solved it
with something called client-side fanout.

89
00:07:10,820 --> 00:07:16,880
Every device gets its own identity key,
and the sender encrypts each message

90
00:07:17,020 --> 00:07:20,520
separately, once for every device you own.

91
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:26,020
The server still holds no key, just
a directory of where those keys live.

92
00:07:27,180 --> 00:07:31,000
And when you link a new laptop, your
history doesn't come from the server.

93
00:07:31,060 --> 00:07:35,640
Your phone encrypts a slice of your recent
chat and hands it over directly to your

94
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:41,860
laptop. And all of this ran on
metal you can point to, not Linux,

95
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:49,440
but FreeBSD, chosen for its mature
TCP stack and a kernel you could tune

96
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:55,400
deep. They bought brick bare metal boxes
and tuned them hard instead of spreading

97
00:07:55,460 --> 00:07:59,720
out lots of small nodes or instead
of doing horizontal scaling.

98
00:08:01,060 --> 00:08:02,900
And they didn't just rent the hardware.

99
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:09,980
They patched the BEAM runtime and the
FreeBSD kernel themselves, hunting down lock

100
00:08:10,119 --> 00:08:15,520
contention. That bought them control,
but it cost them elasticity.

101
00:08:16,340 --> 00:08:23,220
There is no auto-scaling, so you carry
the capacity and the failures yourself.

102
00:08:23,250 --> 00:08:24,450
And there's one last trick.

103
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:31,629
Erlang can load new code into a running
system, so fixes ship without a restart and

104
00:08:31,660 --> 00:08:33,220
the processes never stop.

105
00:08:34,020 --> 00:08:35,840
That's the near zero downtime claim.

106
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:39,560
But resilient is not
the same as invincible.

107
00:08:40,780 --> 00:08:46,340
On the 22nd of February 2014, just three
days after the $19 billion Facebook deal,

108
00:08:46,849 --> 00:08:49,120
WhatsApp went dark for about
three and a half hours.

109
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:52,100
The architecture absorbs failures.

110
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:53,460
It doesn't abolish it.

111
00:08:55,820 --> 00:09:01,830
So that's one message, socket, process,
lookup, handoff, queue, text envelope.

112
00:09:02,380 --> 00:09:07,520
Now, multiply it. A single chat
server doesn't hold one connection.

113
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:14,540
It holds millions. Back in 2011, one
box already held around a million.

114
00:09:15,460 --> 00:09:22,340
By 2012, an engineer named Rick Reed
pushed one past 2 billion at an

115
00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:29,300
Erlang Factory talk. In production, they
ran each near a million on purpose, leaving

116
00:09:29,340 --> 00:09:31,160
headroom for failure and spikes.

117
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:38,780
And the volume climbed right alongside it,
10 billion a day in 2012, 40 billion out

118
00:09:38,860 --> 00:09:43,540
by 2014, roughly 100 billion by 2020.

119
00:09:44,220 --> 00:09:51,140
And the fleet that held the 2014 numbers
was small, around 550 servers, over

120
00:09:51,180 --> 00:09:57,880
11,000 CPU cores, and 147 million
phones connected at once at peak,

121
00:09:58,980 --> 00:10:01,320
which leaves the one question
hanging over all of it.

122
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:03,770
Who was actually running this?

123
00:10:04,570 --> 00:10:09,770
WhatsApp was founded in 2009 on a
deliberately lean do less culture.

124
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:12,340
No ads, no games, no gimmicks.

125
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:15,500
The architecture is that
culture written in code.

126
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:19,260
Roughly 40 million users for
every engineer on the team.

127
00:10:20,220 --> 00:10:26,220
The per user process, the supervisor that
restarts what breaks, the in-RAM lookup,

128
00:10:26,340 --> 00:10:32,240
the blind pipe, every one of those was a
choice that let a tiny team sleep at night.

129
00:10:32,960 --> 00:10:34,840
The leverage wasn't an accident.

130
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:38,440
It was the whole point,
and it wasn't free either.

131
00:10:38,470 --> 00:10:42,400
A team that small is a bus
factor waiting to happen.

132
00:10:43,180 --> 00:10:45,960
The leverage and the limits
were the same decision.

133
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:51,780
40 billion messages a day
ran by a team of roughly 32.

134
00:10:52,760 --> 00:10:56,980
That message you sent earlier without
thinking, it made this exact trip.

135
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:02,679
One socket, one process, one envelope
the server queries but can never open.

136
00:11:05,280 --> 00:11:06,560
One more thing before you go.

137
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:12,200
If taking a system apart like this is your
idea of a good time, please subscribe.

138
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:14,120
That's the whole channel.

139
00:11:14,760 --> 00:11:19,820
And I'm genuinely curious, which of WhatsApp
trades would you have made differently?

140
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:21,380
Tell me in the comments.
